WARNING: the corrector-pass linear solve (velocity and pressure together; gcr= on the per-step line) stalled in 5 solve(s) this run: an iteration could not improve on what it already had, so those solves stopped SHORT of their tolerance and returned that; gcr_res= on the per-step line shows how far short. It means its momentum and pressure sub-solves (gmres=, cg=) made no progress while the residual was still above both gcr.atol and the absolute floor those sub-solves stop at (sqrt(gmres.atol^2 + cg.atol^2), linear_solvers.yaml). Raising gcr.max does not help; tighter gmres.rtol / cg.rtol may. See health.gram_breakdowns in the JSON summary.
WARNING: the outer corrector used all 4 of its solves without converging on 1 of 4 time step(s) (reference = max(the step's predicted-state residual, corrector.atol/corrector.rtol)). Momentum/pressure missed control.yaml corrector.rtol=0.001 / corrector.atol=1e-10 on 1 step(s); the worst step ended at residual/reference=-nan. At least one step ended at or above its reference residual (worst ratio >= 1), so this run may not be converging; check the solution before using it. Stopping short means the nonlinear system was not solved to its tolerance, so the time-accuracy of the run is bounded by that ratio rather than by the tolerance; raise control.yaml corrector.max (or tighten the linear-solver tolerances feeding it) if you need the stated tolerance. See health.corrector_nonconverged, health.corrector_worst_res_ratio and the energy_corrector_* pair in the JSON summary.
WARNING: the outer-corrector residual INCREASED (or became non-finite) on 1 of 4 time step(s): the state a step kept had a larger residual than its own predicted state and than corrector.atol/corrector.rtol (residual/reference > 1), first at step=4; worst residual/reference=-nan. The nonlinear iteration is not converging on those steps. A residual increase alone does not stop the run (only a NaN/Inf does, with exit 1); inspect the solution before using it. A smaller time step (control.yaml dt) is the usual remedy.
WARNING: the pressure sub-solve of the corrector-pass linear solve (cg= on the per-step line) used all 500 of its iterations without reaching its tolerance on 5 solve(s) this run. That sub-solve is what enforces incompressibility, so an under-solved pressure shows up first as the corrector-pass linear solve needing more iterations (gcr=) for the same reduction, and then as a divergence-free constraint satisfied only to the residual the sub-solve stopped at. Raise the cap (linear_solvers.yaml cg.max, currently 500), or loosen the tolerance pair (linear_solvers.yaml cg.rtol / cg.atol, currently 0.1 / 1e-10; echoed as config.linear_solvers.cg.rtol / config.linear_solvers.cg.atol in the JSON summary). The sub-solve checks its residual once every 2 iterations, so a solve that converged inside its final pair is NOT counted here. See health.cg_nonconverged in the JSON summary.
NOTE: the momentum sub-solve of the corrector-pass linear solve (gmres= on the per-step line) used its whole iteration budget (linear_solvers.yaml gmres.max, currently 500) in 10 of its solves this run. This is NOT a fault and is not counted as a non-convergence: the sub-solve only has to reach a relative tolerance of 0.1 (linear_solvers.yaml gmres.rtol), and it is the corrector-pass linear solve around it, not this sub-solve, that drives the residual to gcr.rtol. On well-conditioned cases this count is 0 (measured on cavity, sa_mms, rb_cavity_temp and recirc_bench), so a nonzero value is worth reading as a RATE against a known-good run of the same case: it means the velocity equations got stiffer (mesh quality, dt, viscosity), which normally also shows up as more corrector-pass linear-solve iterations (gcr=). Whether that solve itself stopped short is a different question, answered by health.gcr_nonconverged. See health.gmres_capped in the JSON summary.
